# Line endings, settled once so they stop moving.
#
# Without this, git stores whatever bytes an editor happened to write, so a
# contributor on Windows can flip a file to CRLF and every line in it reads as
# changed. That is not hypothetical here: 8 of 335 text files had drifted to
# CRLF, and normalising 5 of them accounted for roughly 2,200 lines of the
# v3.0.0 diff - noise that buries the ~400 lines anyone actually needed to read.
#
# LF in the repository and LF on checkout, on every platform. Every toolchain
# this project uses (bun, vite, tsc, electron-builder, nginx, Netlify) reads LF
# fine on Windows, and there are no .bat/.cmd scripts, which are the usual
# reason to pin something back to CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, marked so git never inspects or rewrites their bytes. `text=auto`
# detects most of these anyway; naming them makes it explicit rather than
# dependent on a heuristic reading the first few kilobytes.
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.bin   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.sf2   binary
*.docx  binary
